1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:00,975
John Crawfurd:

2
00:00:01,055 --> 00:00:05,575
The Diplomat-Scholar Who Actually
Secured Singapore's Ground

3
00:00:05,575 --> 00:00:09,412
Raffles Place, Raffles Hotel,
Raffles Institution, a university,

4
00:00:09,492 --> 00:00:12,655
an MRT interchange, and a
statue at the spot where he

5
00:00:12,735 --> 00:00:16,572
supposedly first stepped ashore
— Sir Stamford Raffles's name is

6
00:00:16,652 --> 00:00:20,243
stamped across Singapore more
than almost anyone else's. The

7
00:00:20,323 --> 00:00:23,793
man whose actual signature
secured the island for Britain,

8
00:00:23,873 --> 00:00:27,587
on a treaty that ceded it "in
full sovereignty and property...

9
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:31,400
for ever," has a street, a lane
and a bridge named after him.

10
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,200
All three spell his name wrong.

11
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:37,678
What Raffles actually
obtained in February 1819

12
00:00:37,758 --> 00:00:41,538
was permission to "maintain
a factory or factories"

13
00:00:41,618 --> 00:00:45,100
on the island — a trading
post, not territory.

14
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:48,597
The Sultan who signed it,
Hussein, was the elder son of

15
00:00:48,677 --> 00:00:51,914
the previous ruler but had
been passed over for the

16
00:00:51,994 --> 00:00:56,597
throne in favour of his younger
brother, who held it with Dutch backing;

17
00:00:56,677 --> 00:00:58,938
Raffles installed Hussein as a rival

18
00:00:59,018 --> 00:01:02,725
Sultan specifically so he'd
have someone willing to sign.

19
00:01:02,725 --> 00:01:06,874
The Temenggong who co-signed,
a separate figure named Abdul

20
00:01:06,954 --> 00:01:10,458
Rahman who administered
the area around Singapore,

21
00:01:10,538 --> 00:01:15,125
was a subordinate noble of contested
authority in his own right.

22
00:01:15,125 --> 00:01:18,895
The Dutch objected to the
whole arrangement immediately,

23
00:01:18,975 --> 00:01:22,550
and for good reason: none
of it was obviously legal.

24
00:01:22,550 --> 00:01:27,808
The man who actually ran the settlement on
that shaky footing was William Farquhar,

25
00:01:27,888 --> 00:01:30,830
appointed its first
"Resident" — the East India

26
00:01:30,910 --> 00:01:35,203
Company's term for the officer who
governed a settlement day to day,

27
00:01:35,283 --> 00:01:39,126
doubling as magistrate, treasurer
and the Company's political

28
00:01:39,206 --> 00:01:43,450
liaison to whichever local ruler
still nominally held sovereignty.

29
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:46,500
Raffles, based mostly
in Bencoolen with the

30
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:49,194
grander title of Lieutenant-Governor,

31
00:01:49,274 --> 00:01:53,350
was Farquhar's distant superior
and visited only rarely.

32
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:57,260
Farquhar held the Singapore
post for four and a half

33
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:01,557
years before Raffles dismissed
him in 1823, a story with

34
00:02:01,637 --> 00:02:05,550
its own long tail of
injustice — told in full here.

35
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:08,528
What matters for this story is simpler:

36
00:02:08,608 --> 00:02:12,841
when Farquhar left, the
island's legal status was still

37
00:02:12,921 --> 00:02:16,450
exactly as unresolved
as it had been in 1819.

38
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:19,351
His replacement was an unusual choice

39
00:02:19,431 --> 00:02:22,250
for a colonial administrative post.

40
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:27,273
John Crawfurd was born in 1783
on the Scottish island of Islay,

41
00:02:27,353 --> 00:02:30,190
trained as a physician at Edinburgh,

42
00:02:30,270 --> 00:02:34,725
and joined the East India
Company as a surgeon in 1803.

43
00:02:34,725 --> 00:02:39,010
Posted to Penang in 1808, he
taught himself Malay well enough

44
00:02:39,090 --> 00:02:43,231
to become one of the era's
serious scholars of the language

45
00:02:43,311 --> 00:02:47,175
and the region, and it was
there he first met Raffles.

46
00:02:47,175 --> 00:02:50,882
He followed Raffles into
the 1811 invasion of Java

47
00:02:50,962 --> 00:02:54,972
and was appointed Resident
at the court of Yogyakarta,

48
00:02:55,052 --> 00:02:58,456
where he spent years
cultivating relationships

49
00:02:58,536 --> 00:03:02,550
with Javanese aristocrats
and collecting manuscripts.

50
00:03:02,550 --> 00:03:06,447
Returning to Britain in 1816,
he wrote the three-volume

51
00:03:06,527 --> 00:03:10,352
History of the Indian
Archipelago — for years the most

52
00:03:10,432 --> 00:03:14,619
thorough English-language
account of the region available —

53
00:03:14,699 --> 00:03:18,025
and was elected a Fellow
of the Royal Society.

54
00:03:18,025 --> 00:03:22,536
In 1821 he led a diplomatic
mission to Siam and Cochinchina

55
00:03:22,616 --> 00:03:27,516
on behalf of the Governor-General
in Bengal, a difficult posting

56
00:03:27,596 --> 00:03:31,951
that left him experienced in
exactly the kind of patient,

57
00:03:32,031 --> 00:03:36,700
formal negotiation Singapore's
unresolved status now needed.

58
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:41,661
He arrived to take over as the
island's second Resident on the 9th

59
00:03:41,741 --> 00:03:46,778
of June 1823 — a physician, an
Orientalist and a seasoned diplomat,

60
00:03:46,858 --> 00:03:51,975
sent to finish a job Raffles had
started and never actually closed.

61
00:03:51,975 --> 00:03:55,436
Two things happened in 1824 that together

62
00:03:55,516 --> 00:03:58,625
settled Singapore's status for good.

63
00:03:58,625 --> 00:04:02,541
In March, Britain and the
Netherlands signed the Anglo-Dutch

64
00:04:02,621 --> 00:04:05,938
Treaty of London, dividing
the region into separate

65
00:04:06,018 --> 00:04:09,735
spheres of influence — the
Dutch withdrew every objection

66
00:04:09,815 --> 00:04:12,998
to the British holding
Singapore, in exchange for

67
00:04:13,078 --> 00:04:16,875
Britain handing over its own
remaining claims on Sumatra.

68
00:04:16,875 --> 00:04:20,300
That resolved the international dispute.

69
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:25,210
It did nothing to resolve the local
one: Raffles's 1819 arrangement

70
00:04:25,290 --> 00:04:29,976
was still just a trading permission
from a Sultan and Temenggong

71
00:04:30,056 --> 00:04:34,375
of disputed standing, not a
transfer of the island itself.

72
00:04:34,375 --> 00:04:39,475
Crawfurd closed that gap
on the 2nd of August 1824.

73
00:04:39,475 --> 00:04:43,402
In a formal Treaty of Friendship
and Alliance, Sultan Hussein and

74
00:04:43,482 --> 00:04:47,162
Temenggong Abdul Rahman did not
grant a factory — they ceded,

75
00:04:47,242 --> 00:04:51,292
in the treaty's own words, "in full
sovereignty and property to the

76
00:04:51,372 --> 00:04:56,161
Honourable the English East India Company,
their heirs and successors for ever,

77
00:04:56,241 --> 00:04:59,798
the Island of Singapore...
together with the adjacent seas,

78
00:04:59,878 --> 00:05:03,700
straits, and islets, to the
extent of ten geographical miles."

79
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:06,770
A separate article abrogated every earlier

80
00:05:06,850 --> 00:05:09,925
agreement between the two sides outright.

81
00:05:09,925 --> 00:05:14,885
In exchange, the Sultan
received 33,200 Spanish dollars

82
00:05:14,965 --> 00:05:20,199
up front and a lifetime stipend
of 1,300 dollars a month —

83
00:05:20,279 --> 00:05:24,139
more than triple his
old 1819 annual rate —

84
00:05:24,219 --> 00:05:29,625
and the Temenggong received
26,800 dollars and 700 a month.

85
00:05:29,625 --> 00:05:31,914
The Governor-General in Bengal,

86
00:05:31,994 --> 00:05:35,050
Lord Amherst, ratified it that November.

87
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:38,338
It's a dry legal document
next to Raffles's landing,

88
00:05:38,418 --> 00:05:41,383
but it's the one that
would have held up if the

89
00:05:41,463 --> 00:05:44,687
Dutch had ever pressed the
point, and the one every

90
00:05:44,767 --> 00:05:48,200
later British claim to the
island actually rested on.

91
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:51,146
Crawfurd's instructions
were to cut spending,

92
00:05:51,226 --> 00:05:55,180
but he spent anyway, on reclamation
work along the Singapore

93
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:59,025
River that the settlement's
traders had been asking for.

94
00:05:59,025 --> 00:06:02,815
He edited and part-financed
the Singapore Chronicle,

95
00:06:02,895 --> 00:06:07,503
the island's first newspaper,
whose first issue appeared on the

96
00:06:07,583 --> 00:06:11,825
1st of January 1824 — seven
months before his own treaty.

97
00:06:11,825 --> 00:06:15,125
He also moved against slavery
within the settlement:

98
00:06:15,205 --> 00:06:19,545
the following month, in September
1824, he freed a group of enslaved

99
00:06:19,625 --> 00:06:24,030
women who had fled the Sultan's
compound complaining of mistreatment,

100
00:06:24,110 --> 00:06:26,825
part of a broader push
that left Singapore,

101
00:06:26,905 --> 00:06:29,815
unusually for a colonial
free port of the era,

102
00:06:29,895 --> 00:06:34,575
without slavery in any form the local
chiefs tried to maintain it under.

103
00:06:34,575 --> 00:06:39,352
Trade and population kept climbing
through his three years in the post,

104
00:06:39,432 --> 00:06:43,400
building on the momentum
Farquhar had already established.

105
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:46,786
He left Singapore on
the 14th of August 1826,

106
00:06:46,866 --> 00:06:51,408
closing out a tenure of just
over three years, brief even by

107
00:06:51,488 --> 00:06:55,725
the standards of the men who
preceded and followed him.

108
00:06:55,725 --> 00:06:57,799
Crawford Street, Crawford Lane,

109
00:06:57,879 --> 00:07:02,732
Crawford Bridge and Crawford Park
are all that's left of him on a map —

110
00:07:02,812 --> 00:07:05,800
each one spelled with
an "o" he never used.

111
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:09,832
He went on to a long second
career in Britain as a prolific

112
00:07:09,912 --> 00:07:14,014
Orientalist writer and an
outspoken advocate for free trade,

113
00:07:14,094 --> 00:07:18,625
but it's the treaty that has the
better claim on his memory here.

114
00:07:18,625 --> 00:07:22,016
Why it matters today:
Raffles picked the site and

115
00:07:22,096 --> 00:07:25,425
struck the first, legally
shaky bargain for it.

116
00:07:25,425 --> 00:07:28,520
Farquhar spent four and
a half years actually

117
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:31,775
building the settlement
on that shaky ground.

118
00:07:31,775 --> 00:07:35,270
Crawfurd is the one who sat
down with the Sultan and

119
00:07:35,350 --> 00:07:39,532
Temenggong a second time and got
a document that would survive

120
00:07:39,612 --> 00:07:43,107
a legal challenge — full
sovereignty, in perpetuity,

121
00:07:43,187 --> 00:07:47,175
no ambiguity left for the Dutch
or anyone else to exploit.

122
00:07:47,175 --> 00:07:50,875
Singapore remembers the first two names.

123
00:07:50,875 --> 00:07:55,325
The one that closed the deal
ended up on a bridge, misspelled.
